<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
  <b style="font-size: 24px; color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Helping Around the House – Lesson # 1</b><br>
  <i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i>
</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Wash the Dishes Everyday.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions on familiar topics. Interactive: Engage in role-plays based on familiar social situations.
      </p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions about household chores (e.g., "What do you do at home?" "I wash the dishes every day."). Can engage in role-plays based on familiar social situations involving household responsibilities (e.g., "I clean my room on Saturdays."). Can summarize key points about daily chores from discussions (e.g., "Everyone has chores at home.").
      </p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and concepts related to household chores.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or pictures of different household chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "clean the room," "cook"). The teacher asks, "What is this?" or "Do you do this at home?" to activate prior knowledge. Students respond with single words or simple phrases.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation and meaning of core vocabulary (wash the dishes, sweep the floor, clean the room, cook, help) using gestures and realia/pictures. Emphasis is placed on the pronunciation of /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep." The teacher says, "I wash the dishes," "He sweeps the floor," demonstrating the Present Simple. The teacher also introduces simple syllable recognition by saying "cook" (closed) and "do" (open) and having students listen and repeat.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with Concept Checking Questions:
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'washing the floor' correct?" (Expected answer: No, we wash the dishes or mop the floor.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we sweep the table?" (Expected answer: No, we sweep the floor.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'cleaning the room' a chore?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "Helping Around the House."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (MP3 file or teacher-read) between a child (Leo) and his mother discussing Sunday chores. The dialogue includes: "clean my room," "wash the dishes," "cook," "sweep the kitchen floor," and uses "every day" or "usually/often."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b>
          <br>Mom: "Leo, what do you do at home on Sundays?"
          <br>Leo: "On Sundays, I clean my room. I also help you wash the dishes after lunch."
          <br>Mom: "That's great, Leo! I usually cook and sweep the kitchen floor."
          <br>Leo: "And what does Dad do?"
          <br>Mom: "Dad often helps with the laundry."
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions on familiar topics.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (First Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen for general understanding.
              <br>1. "Who is speaking?" (Mom and Leo)
              <br>2. "What are they talking about?" (Chores / Helping at home)
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and mark True (T) or False (F) on their worksheets for statements like:
              <br>1. "Leo cleans the kitchen." (F)
              <br>2. "Mom washes the dishes." (F, Leo helps)
              <br>3. "Dad helps with the laundry." (T)
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listen):</b> Students listen one more time and match family members (Mom, Leo, Dad) to the chores they do (clean room, wash dishes, cook, sweep kitchen floor, help with laundry).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening comprehension and targeted language use.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific vocabulary (dishes, house, chores, floor, kitchen, room) and verbs (wash, clean, sweep, cook, help) within the Present Simple tense and WH-questions ("What do you do at home?") and frequency adverbs ("every day," "often," "usually"). Focus on /w/ and /s/ pronunciation recognition.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Complete (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher plays parts of the audio again, pausing for students to fill in missing words in sentences on a worksheet.
              <br>Example: "On Sundays, I ________ my room." (clean)
              <br>"I usually ________ and ________ the kitchen floor." (cook, sweep)
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher plays short audio clips, each containing a single chore description (e.g., "I wash the dishes every day." "My sister sweeps the floor."). Students listen and point to the corresponding picture flashcard or raise a card with the correct verb. The teacher repeats, "Listen for /w/ in wash and /s/ in sweep."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Check (Pair Interaction):</b> In pairs, students receive a short list of sentences about chores. The teacher reads each sentence aloud (or plays another audio recording). Students listen and circle "Yes" if their list matches what they hear, or "No" if it's different. They then compare answers with their partner to confirm their listening comprehension.
              <br>Example: Teacher says "I clean my room on Saturdays." Students check their lists.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[12-15 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping Around the House."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Listen and Act Out Chores"</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative role-play and mime activity. Students work in pairs.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher first models the activity. The teacher says a chore, e.g., "wash the dishes," and mimes the action. Students listen and watch.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into pairs (Student A and Student B). Each pair receives a small set of picture cards depicting the target chores (wash the dishes, sweep the floor, clean the room, cook, help).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Round 1:</b> Student A picks a chore card and describes the action without saying the chore's name (e.g., "I use water and soap to clean plates." or "I use a broom for the floor."). Student B listens carefully and tries to guess the chore by saying its name (e.g., "You wash the dishes!"). Student B then mimes the action.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Round 2:</b> Students swap roles. Student B describes, Student A listens and guesses, then mimes.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension and appropriate responses. Focus is on fluent comprehension (listening) and basic interaction (speaking).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to gauge students' listening comprehension.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Listen and Act Out Chores" activity and a short listening quiz.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Quiz (Individual):</b> Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 pictures of chores. The teacher reads a sentence for each picture (e.g., "My brother washes the dishes every day."). Students listen and circle the correct picture.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension Questions:</b> The teacher asks simple questions about the main dialogue: "What does Leo do on Sundays?" "Does Mom cook?" Students raise hands to answer.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation and Quiz):</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension of main points:</b> Can identify key chores from spoken sentences.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recognition of target vocabulary:</b> Demonstrates understanding of "wash," "sweep," "clean," "dishes," "floor," "room."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of response:</b> Correctly circles pictures or provides accurate short answers.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Engagement in listening activities:</b> Actively participates in pair work and quiz.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two statements on a small paper slip:
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to understand when people say..." (e.g., "wash the dishes").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was (easy/difficult) to understand the chores today because..."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-8 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words for chores did you understand today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to chores was the most fun?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
          <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? Were students engaged with the audio and visual aids?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in comprehending the main points of the discussions? Were the audio recordings clear and at an appropriate speed for A1.3?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more repetitions, slower speech, simpler vocabulary in subsequent audio) might be needed for the next lesson? How can I ensure better recognition of /w/ and /s/?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job listening to all the chores today! Next lesson, we will use these same chore words and phrases to *read* a short story about a family helping around the house."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>